</math> inches. Each of the two congruent sides of the triangle has a length of <math alttext="24"><mn>24</mn>
</math> inches. What is the length, in inches, of the third side?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["35"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["35"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math alttext="35"><mn>35</mn>
</math>. It&rsquo;s given that the perimeter of an isosceles triangle is <math alttext="83"><mn>83</mn></math> inches and that each of the two congruent sides has a length of <math alttext="24"><mn>24</mn></math> inches. The perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the lengths of its three sides. The equation <math alttext="24 plus 24 plus x equals 83"><mn>24</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>83</mn></math> can be used to represent this situation, where <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> is the length, in inches, of the third side. Combining like terms on the left-hand side of this equation yields <math alttext="48 plus x equals 83"><mn>48</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>83</mn></math>. Subtracting <math alttext="48"><mn>48</mn>
</math> from both sides of this equation yields <math alttext="x equals 35"><mrow>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>35</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Therefore, the length, in inches, of the third side is <math alttext="35"><mn>35</mn></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"997bec28","external_id":"2ed3a51e-14c9-41a5-8246-b0df7d098ee8","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"773fc118-cd33-4e0e-aaac-7c5eea284443","uId":"373bd807-a1c1-4485-93f9-0e12cce12145","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.A.","skill_desc":"Linear equations in one variable","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The perimeter of an isosceles triangle is <math alttext=\"83\"><mn>83</mn>\n</math> inches. Each of the two congruent sides of the triangle has a length of <math alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn>\n</math> inches. What is the length, in inches, of the third side?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["35"],"correct_answer":["35"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"35\"><mn>35</mn>\n</math>. It&rsquo;s given that the perimeter of an isosceles triangle is <math alttext=\"83\"><mn>83</mn></math> inches and that each of the two congruent sides has a length of <math alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn></math> inches. The perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the lengths of its three sides. The equation <math alttext=\"24 plus 24 plus x equals 83\"><mn>24</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>83</mn></math> can be used to represent this situation, where <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> is the length, in inches, of the third side. Combining like terms on the left-hand side of this equation yields <math alttext=\"48 plus x equals 83\"><mn>48</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>83</mn></math>. Subtracting <math alttext=\"48\"><mn>48</mn>\n</math> from both sides of this equation yields <math alttext=\"x equals 35\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>